TOOL=codex
TIMEOUT=1800
CAPTURE_STDOUT=false
CAPTURE_STDOUT_ONLY=false
OUTPUT_FILE=<TMPDIR>/step2b-codex-raw.19602.txt
CMD_JSON=["codex","exec","--sandbox","read-only","-C","<OPERATOR_REPO_PATH>","--add-dir","<OPERATOR_REPO_PATH>","-m","gpt-5.5","-c","projects.\"/Users/zhupanov/larch3\".trust_level=\"trusted\"","-c","model_provider=\"openai-larch-env\"","-c","model_providers.openai-larch-env.name=\"OpenAI API (larch env key)\"","-c","model_providers.openai-larch-env.base_url=\"https://api.openai.com/v1\"","-c","model_providers.openai-larch-env.env_key=\"OPENAI_API_KEY\"","-c","model_providers.openai-larch-env.wire_api=\"responses\"","--output-last-message","<TMPDIR>/step2b-codex-raw.19602.txt","--json","--","You are an expert engineer researching this repository and producing an implementation plan for /design Step 2b.\n\nYou may use only side-effect-free repository discovery. Do not write repository files, design tmpdir files, or any other files. Return only the sentinel-delimited response requested below.\n\nDrafting requirements to follow:\n- Prefer minimum necessary change: avoid scope creep, unnecessary complexity, and additions not required for correctness.\n- Read approach-synthesis.txt: if it is exactly NO_SKETCHES, draft from direct codebase/doc inspection without fabricating planning-panel agreement.\n- Read discussion-round1.md when present for scope boundaries and strict constraints.\n- Read design-outline.md only when non-empty and .outline-approved exists; treat Goals, Non-goals, and Surfaces as binding scope.\n- Read brainstorm.md when present as additive ideation context for plan drafting.\n- Use a Files to modify/create section with per-file headings exactly one path each: ### NEW:, ### UPDATED:, or ### REWRITTEN: (at least one ASCII space after ### before the keyword).\n- Include Approach, Edge cases, Failure modes when non-trivial, Testing strategy, optional diff_added/diff_deleted/mechanical_churn trailers, and final diff_lines: <N>. mechanical_churn accepts only true or false; never write a number there.\n- The final plan body must end with a whole-line diff_lines: <N> trailer.\n- Optionally include up to three dynamic plan-review archetypes in a scout block after the plan. The launcher validates, filters, caps, and materializes this block; invalid post-plan scout output is ignored.\n- Scout sentinels inside the summary or plan are fatal format errors. Never put LARCH_SCOUT_* markers in the plan body.\n\nReadability style (trusted):\n# Readability Style\n\n**Consumer**: `/design` prompt surfaces that produce user-facing text, either through an external-agent prompt token or through inline orchestration prose.\n\n**Contract**: this file is the only source of truth for the `/design` readability preamble. External prompt files embed the literal `<READABILITY_STYLE>` token, and assembly code or orchestration expands it to this full file. Inline composition sites read this file before writing user-facing text.\n\n**When to load**: load only when a `/design` step is about to compose user-facing prose or render a prompt that asks another agent to compose such prose. Do not load during setup, pure validation, or byte-stable artifact handling.\n\n---\n\n## Style Axes\n\nWrite in three styles at once:\n\n- **Strunk & White**: use active voice. Omit needless words. Prefer concrete nouns and verbs.\n- **Dyslexia-friendly**: use short sentences. Prefer simple words. Break dense ideas into headings and bullets.\n- **Brevity**: shorter is better. Minimize the total artifact length while preserving meaning.\n- **No em dashes**: never use em dashes; use periods, commas, colons, or semicolons instead.\n- **Hedge vs. direct**: hedge uncertain claims (\"may contain\", \"can fail\") instead of absolutes; keep directives imperative.\n\n## Precision Contract\n\nKeep these byte-stable unless the task explicitly edits them:\n\n- fenced code blocks\n- backticked tokens\n- file paths\n- identifiers\n- flag names\n- `### NEW:` / `### UPDATED:` / `### REWRITTEN:` plan grammar\n- the trailing `diff_lines: <N>` line\n\nDo not rewrite those items for style. Explain around them instead.\n\n## Plan-Drafting Reminders\n\nWhen `/design` Step 2b drafts a plan that adds, removes, or converts\nBash fences in `skills/implement/SKILL.md`:\n\n- List `### UPDATED: scripts/test-implement-fence-shape.sh` in\n  **Files to modify/create**.\n- Note that `EXPECTED_OLD` / `EXPECTED_NEW` may need updates.\n\nThis is a discoverability reminder only. Do not add plan grammar.\n\n## Precedence\n\nWhen rules conflict, use this order:\n\n`code references > meaning > brevity > dyslexia-friendly chunking > Strunk & White micro-rewrites`\n\nApply it directly:\n\n- Preserve code references first.\n- Preserve exact meaning next.\n- Cut words before adding layout.\n- Add headings or bullets only when they improve scanning.\n- Polish grammar last.\n\n## Substitution Token\n\nExternal-agent prompt files MUST embed this literal token:\n\n`<READABILITY_STYLE>`\n\nBefore launch, replace every literal `<READABILITY_STYLE>` token with the full contents of this file.\n\n## Examples\n\nBefore: \"It is recommended that the implementer should consider adding a validation step.\"\n\nAfter: \"Add a validation step.\"\n\nBefore: \"The plan should make sure that users are not able to continue when the file cannot be read.\"\n\nAfter: \"Block the flow when the file cannot be read.\"\n\nBefore: \"In order to avoid potential future confusion, documentation can be updated.\"\n\nAfter: \"Update the docs to avoid confusion.\"\n\nBefore: \"The script performs an operation that checks if the path exists.\"\n\nAfter: \"The script checks that the path exists.\"\n\nBefore: \"This could possibly be out of scope depending on how the team wants to proceed.\"\n\nAfter: \"This may be out of scope.\"\n\nRequired output format:\n[optional]\nLARCH_SUMMARY_BEGIN\nA concise summary for large-plan preview. Omit this whole summary block only when no useful summary is needed.\nLARCH_SUMMARY_END\n[/optional]\nLARCH_PLAN_BEGIN\nFull implementation plan body ending with diff_lines: <N>.\nLARCH_PLAN_END\n[optional]\nLARCH_SCOUT_BEGIN\n{\"archetypes\":[{\"name\":\"slug\",\"focus_area\":\"code-quality|risk-integration|correctness|architecture|security\",\"weight\":1,\"rationale\":\"single-line reason\",\"prompt_body\":\"2-6 sentence focus directive ending with the required citation sentence.\"}]}\nLARCH_SCOUT_END\n[/optional]\n\nOptional advisory status may be included between LARCH_STATUS_BEGIN and LARCH_STATUS_END, but the summary, plan, and optional scout sentinels above are the only parsed contract.\n\nUntrusted feature description:\n<feature_description encoding=\"literal-redacted\">\n# [BUG] code-review-tally.json reports only round 1 for multi-round /implement runs\n\n## Summary\n\nThe committed `larch-logs/implement/&lt;RUN_ID&gt;/code-review-tally.json` reports `rounds: 1` and an `accepted_count` equal to **round 1's** accepted total, even when the `/implement` Step 5 review loop actually ran multiple rounds. The per-round `round-*/` directories are committed correctly and show the true multi-round results, so the committed run log is internally inconsistent: the round directories say N rounds, the summary tally says 1. The multi-round review loop itself works correctly. This is a reporting/accounting bug only. It propagates into `final-summary.md` (\"Code review: X/Y accepted\") and any consumer that reads the tally (`audit-runs`, `/fluff-analysis`, token/cost summaries), under-reporting how much review work happened. This bug directly caused a misdiagnosis that the `/implement` review panel was producing near-zero accepted findings, when multi-round review was in fact accepting tens of findings per run.\n\n## Original report\n\nRegular `/implement NNNN` runs appear to do only a single review round, and the committed code-review tally shows very low or zero accepted counts. Investigation of committed run logs shows the review loop actually ran multiple rounds with many accepted findings, but `code-review-tally.json` records `rounds: 1` and only round 1's `accepted_count`. The regression appears to start around v50.1.x: every 50.1.x and 51.x regular run records `rounds: 1` regardless of how many `round-*` directories were committed, while older versions recorded multi-round counts correctly.\n\n## Reproduction scenario\n\n1. Run a regular (non-`--emergency`, non-`--self-review`) `/implement &lt;issue&gt;` where Step 5 code review runs 2+ rounds (round 1 accepts findings, coder applies them, the loop continues).\n2. After the run, inspect the committed run log under `larch-logs/implement/&lt;RUN_ID&gt;/`:\n   - Count `round-*` directories (each holds `findings-classification.tsv`, `voting-tally.md`, `review-round-summary.md`).\n   - Read `code-review-tally.json` `rounds` and `accepted_count`.\n3. Observe: number of `round-*` directories is greater than `code-review-tally.json.rounds` (which is `1`), and `accepted_count` equals round 1's accepted total rather than the cumulative across rounds.\n\nConcrete already-committed example: run `03510CED-5FA7-400E-A0E7-568786249E7B` (issue #4533, larch 51.0.0) has five `round-*` directories but `code-review-tally.json` says `rounds: 1, accepted_count: 14`.\n\n## Expected behavior\n\n`code-review-tally.json` should be consistent with the committed `round-*` directories:\n\n- `rounds` equals the total number of review rounds run (= number of committed `round-*` dirs).\n- `accepted_count` / `rejected_count` reflect the documented cumulative-across-rounds semantics (or, if final-round semantics are intended, that should be documented and consistent), not round 1 only.\n- `final-summary.md` \"Code review: X/Y accepted\" should reflect the same totals.\n\n## Observed behavior\n\nFor multi-round regular runs since ~v50.1.x, `code-review-tally.json` is frozen at round 1: `rounds: 1` and `accepted_count` = round 1's accepted total, regardless of how many rounds actually ran. The `round-*` directories committed in the same run log contradict it.\n\n## Root cause analysis\n\nLocated but not fully traced (stated as inference where uncertain):\n\n- `python/review_and_fix.py` `flush_review_batches()` writes `code-review-tally.json` via `voting write-tally --rounds &lt;rounds&gt; --accepted &lt;derived_accepted&gt; ...` with `--mode replace`. `derived_accepted` is computed by `_derive_code_review_tally(findings_file)` over the composed `review-findings-full.jsonl`.\n- The per-round flush inside `_run_round()` calls `flush_review_batches(..., round_num, total_accepted, total_rejected, ...)` every round.\n- The symptom (committed tally frozen at round 1) indicates that rounds 2..N flushes do **not** update the committed `code-review-tally.json`, even though `flush_round_log_after_coder()` commits each `round-*` directory correctly each round. The most likely cause is a flush/commit-ordering interaction: the tally batch is staged in `$IMPLEMENT_TMPDIR/larch-logs` and only the round-1 version lands in the committed repo log, while the round directories are committed incrementally through a different path. This may be compounded by the known in-progress committed-snapshot behavior (runs commit a pre-final snapshot). The exact \"which flush lands in the committed log\" needs confirmation by tracing the commit path for the tally batch versus the round directories.\n\n## Evidence\n\n- Run `03510CED-5FA7-400E-A0E7-568786249E7B` (issue #4533, larch 51.0.0). Five committed round directories, each with real findings + `voting-tally.md`:\n  - round-1: 30 findings, 14 accepted, 3 rejected\n  - round-2: 43 findings, 8 accepted, 5 rejected\n  - round-3: 27 findings, 11 accepted, 9 rejected\n  - round-4: 29 findings, 11 accepted, 5 rejected\n  - round-5: 25 findings, 15 accepted, 2 rejected\n  - Total: ~59 accepted across 5 rounds. But `code-review-tally.json` says `rounds: 1, accepted_count: 14` (= round 1 only).\n- Other recent committed runs (tally `rounds` vs actual `round-*` dir count): issue #4170 = 3 dirs but `rounds=1`; #4016 = 2 dirs but `rounds=1`; #4427 = 2 dirs but `rounds=1`. Issue #4503 = 2 dirs and `rounds=2` (correct), so it is not 100% always wrong.\n- Regression window (distribution of `code-review-tally.json.rounds` for regular runs, by version era; \"multi-round %\" = share of runs with `rounds &gt; 1`):\n  - `&lt;=40`: 48%, `42-45.x`: 40%, `47.x`: 57%, `49.x`: 53%, `50.0.x`: 30%\n  - `50.1.x`: 0% (9 runs, all `rounds=1`), `51.x`: 8% (only 1 of 12 with `rounds &gt; 1`)\n  - Older eras reported multi-round correctly; starting ~v50.1.x the field is effectively pinned at 1. Bisecting commits landing in v50.1.0 / v50.1.x that touched the Step 5 tally flush path is the fastest way to localize.\n\n## Affected files\n\n- `python/review_and_fix.py` — `flush_review_batches()` (writes `code-review-tally.json`), the per-round flush calls inside `_run_round()`, and the terminal Step 5 flush callers. Primary suspect for the round-count and accepted-count freezing.\n- `python/voting.py` — `write-tally` verb (records `rounds`/`accepted`/`rejected` into the tally JSON). Consumer of the (incorrect) `--rounds` / `--accepted` values.\n- `docs/run-logs.md` — `code-review-tally.json` `rounds` / `accepted_count` semantics; should be made explicit (cumulative vs final-round) so the fix has a contract to satisfy.\n- `skills/implement/scripts/write-final-report.md` / final-summary rendering — reads the tally and prints \"Code review: X/Y accepted\"; inherits the under-report.\n\n## Suggested fix(es)\n\n- Ensure the terminal Step 5 flush re-writes `code-review-tally.json` with the cumulative `rounds_completed` and cumulative accepted/rejected, and that this terminal flush is the copy that lands in the committed log (re-commit the tally batch after the loop completes, not only after round 1).\n- Add a regression assertion: for a 2+ round run, committed `code-review-tally.json.rounds` equals the number of committed `round-*` directories, and `accepted_count` matches the cumulative round summaries.\n- Decide and document the intended `accepted_count` semantics for multi-round runs (cumulative across rounds vs final round) in `docs/run-logs.md`, then make the writer conform.\n\n## Open questions\n\n- Intended semantics of `accepted_count` for multi-round runs: cumulative across all rounds, or the final round only? The fix and the regression test depend on this decision.\n- Is the freeze caused by the per-round flush staging vs the commit path (tally batch not re-committed after round 1), or by a stale `rounds` argument passed at the terminal flush? Needs a trace of one live multi-round run to confirm.\n- Does the same round/accepted under-reporting affect `/review` (standalone) tallies, or only `/implement` Step 5?\n\n\n</feature_description>\n\n\nUntrusted approach synthesis:\n<approach_synthesis encoding=\"literal-redacted\">\nNO_SKETCHES\n\n</approach_synthesis>\n\n\nUntrusted discussion round 1:\n<discussion_round1 encoding=\"literal-redacted\">\n## Decision 1: code-review-tally.json multi-round semantics\n- **Question**: For multi-round runs, should accepted_count / rejected_count / rounds be cumulative across rounds or final-round only?\n- **Resolution**: Cumulative. `rounds` = total rounds run (= committed `round-*` dir count); `accepted_count` / `rejected_count` = totals across all rounds. Document this contract in `docs/run-logs.md`. Matches the already-correct `review-findings-full.jsonl` and the code's de-facto derivation (`_derive_code_review_tally` over the composed all-rounds findings).\n- **Source**: user\n\n## Decision 2: Fix scope across skills\n- **Question**: Fix only /implement Step 5, or also explicitly cover standalone /review?\n- **Resolution**: Fix the shared `voting write-tally` writer (structurally corrects both /implement and /review). Add a regression test for the /implement Step 5 multi-round case only. Standalone /review benefits automatically; no separate /review test required.\n- **Source**: user\n\n## Decision 3: Historical committed tallies (non-goal)\n- **Question**: Should already-committed wrong `code-review-tally.json` files be backfilled / corrected?\n- **Resolution**: No. Historical `larch-logs` are immutable run records; backfilling is out of scope. Consumers (`audit-runs`, `/fluff-analysis`, token/cost summaries) that read historical tallies are not modified by this fix.\n- **Source**: codebase / orchestrator\n\n## Scope boundaries and hard constraints\n- **In-scope**: make the code-review tally write resilient so multi-round runs commit correct cumulative `rounds` / `accepted_count` / `rejected_count`; document the semantics; add a /implement multi-round regression test.\n- **Hard constraints**: do NOT change plan-review tally behavior (plan-review STORES the body, so its header validation is meaningful and must stay); preserve the code-review-tally record schema (`schema_version` 2; `phase` / `batch` / `mode` / `rounds` / `accepted_count` / `rejected_count` / `exonerated_count`); keep `review-findings-full.jsonl` behavior unchanged (already correct).\n- **Non-goals**: backfilling historical logs; changing tally consumers; expanding the /review test surface; redesigning the flush/commit pipeline.\n\n</discussion_round1>\n\n\nUntrusted approved design outline:\n<design_outline encoding=\"literal-redacted\">\n## Proposed Design Outline\n\n### Goals\n- Report true multi-round results in `code-review-tally.json`: `rounds` = committed `round-*` count; `accepted_count` / `rejected_count` = cumulative across rounds.\n- Stop the tally write from silently freezing at round 1.\n- Document the cumulative semantics as a shared contract.\n\n### Non-goals\n- Backfilling already-committed historical tallies.\n- Changing tally consumers (`audit-runs`, `/fluff-analysis`, token/cost summaries).\n- A separate standalone `/review` test (the shared-writer fix covers it).\n- Touching the `review-findings-full.jsonl` path (already correct).\n\n### Approach sketch\n- Root cause: `voting write-tally` calls `_die` on code-review body header-validation failure before writing; the body is discarded for code-review, so the gate only harms.\n- Make code-review tally-body validation non-fatal: warn, then write the record. Keep the existing cumulative count derivation and the explicit round count.\n- Leave the plan-review tally path untouched (it stores and uses its body).\n- Add a 2+ round regression test: written tally `rounds` == round-dir count and counts == cumulative, even when a body line trips the old gate.\n\n### Surfaces in scope\n- `python/voting.py` — `write_tally_main` code-review validation gate.\n- `docs/run-logs.md` — cumulative-semantics contract.\n- `python/test_voting.py`, `python/test_review_and_fix.py` — regression coverage.\n\n### Open questions\n- None.\n\n</design_outline>\n\n"]
OUTER_LAUNCHER=agent launch-codex-exec
OUTER_LAUNCHER_PROMPT_FILE=<TMPDIR>/step2b-codex-raw.19602.txt.prompt
OUTER_LAUNCHER_WORKDIR=<OPERATOR_REPO_PATH>
OUTER_LAUNCHER_KIND=codex-exec
OUTER_LAUNCHER_SANDBOX=read-only
OUTER_LAUNCHER_WITH_EFFORT=false
OUTER_LAUNCHER_USAGE_LABEL=codex_plan_draft
OUTER_LAUNCHER_TIMING_KIND=codex-plan-draft
OUTER_LAUNCHER_ADD_DIRS_JSON=["/Users/zhupanov/larch3"]
